Get or set a resource limit

#include <sys/resource.h>
iinntt ggeettrrlliimmiitt((resource, rlp))
iinntt sseettrrlliimmiitt((resource, rlp))
int resource; struct rlimit *rlp;

Each process has a soft and a hard limit, rlim_cur and rlim_max,
for each resource.  getrlimit copies them to *rlp; setrlimit sets
them from it.  The soft limit may be anything up to the hard limit.
Any process may lower its hard limit; only the superuser may raise
it.  fork and exec keep the limits.  RLIM_INFINITY means as much as
the system can give.

The C900 kernel keeps only RLIMIT_STACK; the other resources are
refused with EINVAL.  exec gives a program a stack of the soft
limit, committed whole, because a Z8001 stack cannot grow on demand.
A program that goes deeper dies of SIGSEGV.  The soft limit starts
at 16 KB; the hard limit starts at 62 KB, the largest stack segment
the MMU maps.  The shell's "ulimit -s" sets them in kilobytes.

The arguments and environment are part of the stack and may fill
all of it but 4 KB; past that exec fails with E2BIG.  So the
argument space follows the stack limit: about 12 KB at the default,
about 28 KB with "ulimit -s 32".

***** See Also *****

COHERENT system calls, exec, sh

***** Diagnostics *****

Both return 0 on success and -1 on error: EINVAL for an unknown
resource or a soft limit above the hard one, EPERM for a hard limit
raised by an ordinary user, EFAULT for a bad rlp.
